{"id":"berries_picking","decision":"Cold organic berries with a dry, clean carton bottom.","cart_pick":"Fresh berries","tier_note":"Strawberries, blackberries and blueberries sit high on the residue list from USDA testing, whatever the container looks like.","buying_tips":["The bottom berries carry the weight and mold first, while the top still looks fresh.","A dry, unstained bottom on the carton. That is the whole check.","No fuzzy white or gray spot on any berry. One takes the punnet.","Green caps still fresh on strawberries, not browned and dry.","Deep, even color for the variety, and uniform size in the container.","Buy them cold, from the refrigerated display rather than a room-temperature stack.","No USDA Organic seal on the clamshell means conventional, whatever the box says.","Read the seal, not the photograph; the two cartons look almost identical.","Frozen organic strawberries: same seal and same rules."]}
{"id":"sprouts_raw","decision":"Cold-case sprouts with white roots and crisp stems.","cart_pick":"Broccoli sprouts","tier_note":"Sprouts go back centuries with little modern study, and buying them cold is a freshness standard, not a health claim.","buying_tips":["Warm, damp growing conditions also multiply any bacteria already on the seed.","The refrigerated case only. Room-temperature sprouts have been left in growing conditions.","Roots still white and threadlike, stems crisp rather than limp.","No condensation fogging the clamshell and no sour smell through the vents.","A pack date you can read. Days old, not a week.","Or a jar, a mesh lid and a scoop of seed. Three days on the counter."]}
{"id":"produce_root_vegetables","decision":"Firm organic potatoes, conventional carrots, with nothing soft or sprouting.","cart_pick":"Firm roots with no sprouts or soft spots","tier_note":"Potatoes rank high for residue in USDA testing and carrots rank low, so the bag decides.","buying_tips":["Carrots rank among the lowest for pesticide residue, so conventional is fine there.","Potatoes: firm, with no bruises, nicks, or soft spots.","Carrots: firm and well colored. With the tops on, choose bright green, fresh-looking tops.","Sweet potatoes: smooth and firm, even in shape and size, peel color even for the variety, free of damage.","Beets: firm and round with a slender tap root. The leaves on top say nothing certain about the root. Wilted leaves say little as long as the root is firm.","Potatoes: a cool, dark, ventilated place, 45 to 50°F, for up to a month.","Carrots: a closed bag or container in the refrigerator, tops removed, up to two weeks. A wilted carrot revives in cold water.","Beets: stem trimmed to an inch, root end left on, refrigerated in a food-safe container up to two weeks."]}
{"id":"produce_brassicas","decision":"Conventional cabbage and cauliflower, tight heads, no soft spots.","cart_pick":"Firm broccoli, cauliflower, or cabbage","tier_note":"Cabbage and cauliflower both sit on the low-residue list from USDA testing.","buying_tips":["Cabbage and cauliflower rank among the lowest produce for pesticide residue.","Broccoli: dark green florets, closed tight, on stems that are not thick and tough.","Cauliflower: a head with no bruises or damage.","Cabbage: outer leaves fresh, not wilted or insect-damaged. Remove any that are.","Broccoli keeps in the refrigerator in a plastic bag with holes. Use it within three days.","Cabbage keeps several weeks in a plastic bag or wrap in the refrigerator, at 41°F or below.","Cut out the cabbage core and rinse the leaves under cool running water before using."]}
{"id":"produce_onions_garlic","decision":"Conventional onions and garlic, firm with a dry neck.","cart_pick":"Firm dry onions and garlic with dried necks","tier_note":"Onions sit on the low-residue list from USDA testing, and the papery skin comes off.","buying_tips":["A dry neck and papery skins mark a well-cured bulb; soft or scented ones are fading.","Onions firm for their size, with little to no scent.","An onion neck that is completely dry to the touch, not slippery.","Garlic well cured: dried neck, dry outer skins, cloves firm to the touch.","Some loose skins on an onion are normal.","Whole onions: cool, dry, dark, with air moving. Never in plastic.","Cut onions go in a sealed container for up to seven days."]}
{"id":"produce_leafy_greens","decision":"Crisp organic greens, evenly colored, without wilt or yellowing.","cart_pick":"Crisp greens with no yellowing or wilt","tier_note":"Spinach and kale top the high-residue list from USDA testing, and the leaves get eaten whole.","buying_tips":["By weight, spinach carries more pesticide residue than any other produce.","Lettuce: crisp leaves, bright to dark green outside and pale at the heart, free of decay or damage.","Romaine: a head that is neither very loose nor very hard. Loose is immature; hard is overmature.","Spinach: uniformly green, fully crisp, fairly clean.","Kale: firm, deep colored leaves on moist, hardy stems. Smaller leaves are more tender and milder.","Store lettuce and spinach as close to 32°F as the refrigerator gets.","Kale: plastic bag, air pressed out, unwashed, about five days in the refrigerator."]}
{"id":"produce_peppers","decision":"Firm organic peppers, evenly colored, without cracks or soft spots.","cart_pick":"Firm, unbruised bell peppers","tier_note":"The skin of a pepper gets eaten, so the skin rule puts peppers in organic.","buying_tips":["A firm pepper still holds its water; wrinkled skin means it dried out in storage.","Firm, with uniform shape, size, and color for the variety.","Colored peppers: at least half of the surface colored.","Free of cracks, decay, sunburn, disease, or insect damage.","Skip a bruised or damaged pepper.","Store around 45°F where possible; at 41°F, plan on about two weeks.","Red, yellow, and orange peppers tolerate the cold better than green."]}
{"id":"produce_apples_pears","decision":"Unbruised organic apples and pears, with no spoiled fruit alongside.","cart_pick":"Firm, unbruised apples or pears","tier_note":"USDA testing finds more residue on apples and pears, and their skin gets eaten.","buying_tips":["Bruised fruit makes the sound fruit beside it ripen and rot faster.","Apples: firm and crisp, free of bruising, decay, cracks, and insect injury.","Pears: free of bruising, insect damage, and decay; a firm green Bartlett ripens off the tree.","Store in the dark at 30 to 32°F, up to 40°F, in a ventilated box or a perforated bag.","Do not wash before storing; the moisture hastens decay.","McIntosh and Honeycrisp: store at 38°F, since they are prone to chilling injury.","Refrigerated apples keep up to a month."]}
{"id":"produce_citrus","decision":"Firm, smooth citrus: conventional to eat, organic for zest.","cart_pick":"Firm, smooth oranges, lemons, or limes","tier_note":"USDA tests oranges with the peel off, so organic only earns its place when the zest goes in.","buying_tips":["A partly green orange can be fully mature, so firmness and a smooth rind tell more.","Oranges: even color, firm, smooth, no decay, bruising, or blemishes.","Lemons: even yellow, firm, smooth, no drying, rind stains, or shriveling.","Limes: firm and smooth, no bruises, oil spotting, or dryness; yellow ones have more juice.","Oranges: room temperature up to a week, refrigerator up to three weeks.","Lemons and limes hold best in the 50s °F, warmer than the refrigerator.","Use the yellowed lemons and limes first; the green-picked ones keep longest."]}
{"id":"produce_stone_fruit","decision":"Unbruised organic peaches, nectarines, plums and cherries, color fully turned.","cart_pick":"Unbruised peaches, plums, or cherries","tier_note":"USDA testing finds more residue on peaches, nectarines and cherries, all eaten skin-on.","buying_tips":["A bruise stays for good, and peaches and cherries both make the Dirty Dozen.","Peaches and nectarines: ground color gone from green to yellow, no bruises or damage.","Plums: skin color turned for the variety, no bruises or damage.","Cherries: full color across the surface, green fleshy stems, no cracks, pecks, or shriveling.","Store just above freezing, 30.5 to 32°F; cherries at or below 41°F.","Plums that still need to ripen: keep them out of the 36 to 46°F range.","Skin staining on a peach or nectarine is cosmetic; a bruise is not."]}
{"id":"produce_mushrooms","decision":"Dry, firm conventional mushrooms with no dark spots.","cart_pick":"Dry, firm mushrooms with no dark spots","tier_note":"Mushrooms sit on the low-residue list from USDA testing, so plain ones earn the cart.","buying_tips":["Moisture is what spoils mushrooms, so a dry, firm cap is the fresh one.","Feel for a dry, firm cap and pass on any with dark spots.","Pick closed caps for a mild flavor and open caps for a stronger one.","Buy them loose to take only the amount needed."]}
{"id":"produce_bananas","decision":"Green bananas to finish ripening on your counter.","cart_pick":"Green or just-turning bananas","tier_note":"Conventional is fine on green bananas: they rank low for residue, tested with the peel off.","buying_tips":["Bananas get sweeter as the green fades, and ripening on the plant often splits them.","Take the greenest bunch for the week; it keeps ripening at home.","Keep green bananas out of the fridge. Cold below 56°F can stop them ripening.","Pass on fruit with skin splits or dark streaks."]}
{"id":"produce_green_beans","decision":"Smooth, crisp, slender organic beans, without bulging seeds.","cart_pick":"Crisp, slender green beans","tier_note":"The whole pod gets eaten, so green beans follow the skin rule to organic.","buying_tips":["Bulging seeds mean an older bean, turning tough, stringy and starchy.","Pick smooth, slender pods with no seeds bulging through.","Leave beans with brown or rust spots behind.","Store them cold and cook them within five days."]}
